== 3.18 Multi-Tier Cross-Modality Response Prediction Across 13 References and 15 Bulk Cohorts
While self-reference pseudobulk validation (Section 3.12) and first-principles synthetic benchmarks (Sections 3.14–3.17) establish the theoretical operating regimes of bulk deconvolution, clinical translation requires determining whether fine-grained cell-state atlases derived from independent single-cell cohorts predict checkpoint blockade outcomes in unfractionated clinical trial bulk RNA-seq. Using the multi-stage pipeline orchestrated via `tme_datasets` (Section 2.13, @fig-tme-datasets-pipeline-dag), we deconvolved 11 clinical bulk RNA-seq trials and 4 indication/pan-cancer pooled strata ($N = 1,058$ evaluable pre-treatment patients; 9 CRI iAtlas trials totaling $N = 1,015$ plus 2 anti-PD-(L)1 NSCLC cohorts totaling $N = 43$) against all 13 hierarchical single-cell references ($3.60 times 10^6$ cells, 355 Leiden $r = 2.0$ states across Tiers 1A, 1B, 2, and 3; Section 2.14) and evaluated 5 supervised classifier families under $5 times 5$ repeated stratified cross-validation (@fig-iatlas-roc-auc, @fig-iatlas-prauc).

Across the $13 times 15 = 195$ reference-by-cohort combinations, collinearity-resolved `Consensus-Meta42` ($K = 42$ Ward-clustered pan-cancer meta-states) consistently matched or outperformed both cohort-specific Tier 1A references and the uncollapsed 355-state `Unified-All9` concatenation (@fig-iatlas-roc-auc a). Averaged across all 15 bulk cohorts and pooled strata, `Consensus-Meta42` achieved a mean out-of-fold `ROC-AUC` of $0.713$ under Ridge Linear Probability Modeling (`linear_reg`), $0.689$ under $ell_2$ Logistic Regression (`ridge_l2`), and $0.687$ under Elastic-Net (`elastic_net`), surpassing uncollapsed `Unified-All9` ($0.666$, $0.662$, and $0.664$, respectively) by $+0.027$ to $+0.047$ `ROC-AUC` units. This empirical gain directly validates our theoretical collinearity analysis (Sections 2.8 and 3.14): concatenating 9 single-cell atlases without consolidating duplicate lineages inflates predictor dimensionality ($K = 355 > N$ in every single trial) and injects severe negative cross-talk between near-identical T-cell and myeloid states across studies, whereas Ward clustering onto $K = 42$ consensus meta-states restores conditioning ($kappa << 100$) while preserving specialized microenvironmental niches across 7 solid tumor types.

Within individual clinical trials and pooled disease strata on `Consensus-Meta42` (@fig-iatlas-roc-auc b), compositional predictors achieved strong discrimination in anti-PD-1 NSCLC (`Cho` @cho2020genome: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.945$ for `ridge_l2`, $0.927$ for `linear_reg`, $0.909$ for `random_forest`; `NSCLC-Combined`, $n = 43$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.782$ for `ridge_l2`, $0.769$ for `linear_reg`, $0.767$ for `random_forest`) and metastatic melanoma (`Hugo` @hugo2016genomic, $n = 27$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.769$ for `linear_reg`, $0.720$ for `ridge_l2`; `Gide` @gide2019clinical, $n = 91$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.764$ for `linear_reg`, $0.750$ for `ridge_l2`, $0.740$ for `random_forest`; `Riaz` @riaz2017tumor, $n = 98$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.674$ for `linear_reg`, $0.646$ for `ridge_l2`). In larger, histologically heterogeneous trials and multi-center pools, `Consensus-Meta42` maintained statistically significant discrimination across urothelial bladder cancer (`Rosenberg` IMvigor210 @rosenberg2016atezolizumab, $n = 298$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.641$), pooled melanoma (`Melanoma-Combined`, $n = 338$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.628$ for `random_forest`, $0.622$ for `ridge_l2`), pooled renal cell carcinoma (`RenalCell-Combined`, $n = 263$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.620$), and the complete 9-trial pan-cancer cohort (`PanCancer-Combined`, $N = 1,015$: `ROC-AUC` $= 0.619$ for `ridge_l2` and `elastic_net`, $0.618$ for `linear_reg`, $p < 10^(-6)$).

Because objective clinical response rates in checkpoint blockade trials are frequently below $35%$ (`Rosenberg` $pi_("pos") = 0.228$, `Riaz` $0.204$, `McDermott` $0.259$, `PanCancer-Combined` $0.314$), `ROC-AUC` alone can obscure false-positive inflation in precision–recall space. Evaluating `PR-AUC` alongside prevalence-calibrated excess precision $Delta "PR-AUC" = "PR-AUC" - pi_("pos")$ (@fig-iatlas-prauc) confirmed substantial positive precision lift across every disease indication. On `Consensus-Meta42`, mean `PR-AUC` across all 15 cohorts reached $0.589$ ($Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.260$) for `linear_reg` and $0.542$ ($Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.213$) for `ridge_l2`, outperforming `Unified-All9` ($0.520$, $Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.191$) by $+6.9$ percentage points of excess precision. In `PanCancer-Combined` ($N = 1,015$), compositional models lifted `PR-AUC` from the $0.314$ prevalence baseline to $0.423$ ($Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.109$), while reaching `PR-AUC` $= 0.903$ ($Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.590$) in `Cho`, $0.822$ ($Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.273$) in `Gide`, $0.761$ ($Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.279$) in `Hugo`, and $0.684$ ($Delta "PR-AUC" = +0.358$) in `NSCLC-Combined`.


== 3.19 Pan-Cohort Linearized Deconvolution Concordance with Single-Cell Milopy Abundance and Zero-Shot LOCO Transfer
A central methodological question of this study is whether cell-state responder associations inferred by bulk RNA-seq deconvolution ($hat(beta)_("deconv", k)$) in independent clinical trials agree directionally and quantitatively with continuous single-cell neighborhood differential abundance (`milopy` $overline("logFC")_k$) measured directly in patient biopsies. Crucially, after enforcing automated $log_2$-scale detection and exact linear TPM inversion ($2^X - 1 -> 10^6$ TPM), whole-tumor stromal background alignment for sorted leukocyte references, and cell-size $S_k$ correction (Section 2.14), cross-modality concordance between DerSimonian–Laird meta-analytic bulk effects (`Pan-iAtlas-Meta`, $N = 1,015$) and single-cell `milopy` $overline("logFC")_k$ was remarkably strong across all 13 references (@fig-main-combined[panels c, d of Figure], @fig-iatlas-transfer-loco).

Across all 13 single-cell references evaluated against `Pan-iAtlas-Meta` (@fig-main-combined[panel d of Figure]), Pearson linear correlation $r$ ranged from $+0.886$ (`CELLxGENE_6f9de485` TNBC @yan2026singlecell, Spearman $rho = +0.830$, $83.3%$ sign agreement) to $+0.987$ (`GSE233203` NSCLC @hong2025singlecell, $rho = +0.962$, $100.0%$ sign agreement across 34/34 states; all 13 references $p <= 5 times 10^(-4)$ by exact permutation test). Notable single-cohort and unified concordance benchmarks included:
- *Lung & Colorectal Tier 1A Atlases*: `GSE207422` @hu2023singlecell achieved $r = +0.980, rho = +0.983$ ($97.4%$ sign agreement, 37/38 states), `GSE243013` @liu2025multiomic achieved $r = +0.940, rho = +0.917$ ($90.9%$, 30/33), and `CELLxGENE_05a8c945` @marteau2026singlecell achieved $100.0%$ directional sign concordance (39/39 states, $r = +0.931, rho = +0.912$).
- *Melanoma, Pan-Cancer, HNSCC & PDAC Tier 1A Atlases*: `CELLxGENE_7b20c613` @gondal2025curated achieved $r = +0.962, rho = +0.940$ ($95.8%$ sign agreement, 46/48), `GSE120575` @sadefeldman2018defining achieved $r = +0.959, rho = +0.928$ ($84.2%$, 32/38), `GSE200996` @luoma2022molecular achieved $r = +0.963, rho = +0.933$ ($86.2%$, 25/29), and `GSE316195` @raufi2026singlenucleus achieved $r = +0.947, rho = +0.879$ ($94.4%$, 51/54).
- *Unified Multi-Cohort Tiers (1B, 2, and 3)*: `NSCLC-Unified` ($K = 105$) achieved $r = +0.969, rho = +0.929$ ($90.5%$ sign agreement, 95/105), `Melanoma-PanCancer-Unified` ($K = 86$) achieved $r = +0.958, rho = +0.948$ ($88.4%$, 76/86), `Unified-All9` ($K = 355$) achieved $r = +0.963, rho = +0.925$ ($85.9%$, 305/355), and the 42-state pan-cancer `Consensus-Meta42` reference achieved $r = +0.928$, Spearman $rho = +0.917$, and *$88.1%$ (37/42 states) directional sign concordance* ($p = 5 times 10^(-4)$).

Inspecting the complete $13 times 16$ cross-cohort transfer matrix (@fig-iatlas-transfer-loco a) demonstrated that responder/non-responder TME axes discovered in single-cell atlases transfer positively across nearly all independent bulk trials, with expected attenuation only in tiny single-arm cohorts ($n < 30$) or heavily desmoplastic pancreatic cancer (`Padron`). Crucially, rigorous zero-shot *Leave-One-Cohort-Out (`LOCO`)* testing across the 11 clinical trials (@fig-iatlas-transfer-loco b) revealed an instructive divergence between within-cohort and cross-trial model inductive biases: whereas regularized linear classifiers (`linear_reg`, `ridge_l2`) slightly led within-cohort CV on small trials due to lower variance, non-linear *`Random Forest` achieved the highest zero-shot `LOCO` generalization* across held-out clinical trials on `Consensus-Meta42` (mean `LOCO ROC-AUC` $= 0.661$, mean `LOCO PR-AUC` $= 0.526$, vs. $0.639$ for `linear_reg` and $0.618$ for `ridge_l2`), reaching zero-shot `LOCO ROC-AUC` $= 0.945$ in `Cho`, $0.779$ in `Gide` (surpassing within-cohort CV $0.740$), $0.744$ in `Choueiri`, and $0.730$ in `Jung`. Because `LOCO` pools $N approx 930–1,040$ training samples across 10 trials, tree ensembles acquire sufficient sample support to learn invariant non-linear threshold rules that transfer across sequencing centers without study-specific recalibration.

To systematically dissect the computational principles governing reference construction across diverse cancer types, we benchmarked 40+ hierarchical and combinatorial single-cell references across clustering resolution, state granularity regimes, multi-cohort pooling depth, and subsampling fractions (@fig-sc9-reference-summary, @fig-supp-sc9-fig1, @fig-supp-sc9-fig2, @fig-supp-sc9-fig3, @fig-supp-sc9-fig4, @fig-supp-sc9-fig5, @fig-supp-meta42-envelope, @fig-supp-meta42-mechanisms, @fig-supp-meta42-construction, @fig-supp-meta42-atlas). Across all evaluated configurations, out-of-cohort predictive capacity scaled monotonically with single-cell clustering resolution ($"res" = 0.25$ to $2.0$, Pearson $r = +0.439, p = 1.2 times 10^(-14)$, Spearman $rho = +0.445, p = 4.7 times 10^(-15)$; @fig-sc9-reference-summary a) and cell-state dictionary granularity ($<15$ to $>= 40$ states, $r = +0.640, p = 1.1 times 10^(-33)$, $rho = +0.646, p = 1.6 times 10^(-34)$; @fig-sc9-reference-summary b), with Random Forest variance decomposition confirming that state granularity ($54.8%$) and matrix condition number ($29.9%$) dominate predictive variance (@fig-supp-meta42-envelope c) and define a safe operating corridor ($kappa <= 40$, $"AUC" >= 0.70$; @fig-supp-meta42-mechanisms a). This performance lift plateaued in the $35–45$ state regime, balancing functional sub-phenotype resolution against signature collinearity inflation (@fig-supp-sc9-fig2, @fig-supp-sc9-fig3, @fig-supp-meta42-mechanisms c–e). Furthermore, integrating single-cell atlases across multiple cohorts ($K = 1 dots 9$) significantly improved multi-trial generalization ($r = +0.251, p = 2.1 times 10^(-5)$; @fig-sc9-reference-summary c, @fig-supp-sc9-fig5) and motivated our five-stage Ward linkage assembly of the 42-state `Consensus-Meta42` reference atlas from 355 local single-cell clusters (@fig-supp-meta42-construction, @fig-supp-meta42-atlas), which achieved the highest pan-cancer precision–recall and receiver operating characteristic performance across all 32 evaluated transcriptomic, genomic, and composite biomarkers ($"Mean PR-AUC" = 0.525$ $[0.465, 0.585]$ vs. $0.314$ prevalence baseline, and $"Mean ROC-AUC" = 0.720$ $[0.669, 0.771]$ vs. $0.50$ random baseline; @fig-sc9-reference-summary[panels d, e of Figure]), demonstrating that multi-cohort reference harmonization is essential for eliminating batch-specific distortions and delivering robust clinical biomarker signatures.


== 3.20 Compositional Effect-Size Architecture and Marker Gene Specificity Across Focused and Complete 42-State Atlases
To dissect the biological programs driving `Consensus-Meta42` performance at both high-level pole resolution and exhaustive pan-atlas completeness, we paired random-effects forest plots and multi-trial effect heatmaps with top-4 protein-coding HGNC marker specificity matrices across (i) the focused 14 extreme responder/non-responder poles (@fig-main-combined[panel f of Figure], @fig-metastate-deg-14[panel a of Figure]), (ii) a 12-spoke compartment-balanced pan-cancer radar immunogram across observed clinical `RECIST` responder profiles (@fig-metastate-deg-14[panel b of Figure]), cohort strata, $2 times 2$ stromal-gating regimes, and individual patient exemplars (@fig-meta42-responder-radar, @fig-meta42-patient-exemplars-radar), and (iii) all 42 `Consensus-Meta42` meta-states (`M01`–`M42`, `168` state-gene pairs; @fig-metastate-forest-all42, @fig-metastate-deg-all42).

At the responder pole (@fig-main-combined[panel f of Figure]), checkpoint sensitivity across solid tumors is anchored by a coordinated four-compartment cellular triad-plus-humoral niche:
1. *Clonally Expanded Exhausted/Tumor-Reactive $"CD8"^+$ T Cells (`M13: CXCL13+PDCD1+ Tex`)*: Strongest pan-cancer responder enrichment in both bulk trials ($hat(beta)_("meta") = +2.70$) and single-cell neighborhoods (`milopy` $overline("logFC") = +6.96$), anchored by top-4 reference markers _CXCL13_ ($log_2 "FC"_("spec") = +2.39$, bulk responder Welch $t = +5.11$), _PDCD1_ ($+1.87, t = +4.31$), _NAPSA_ ($+2.23, t = +2.43$), and _PLAU_ ($+1.80, t = +2.23$) (@fig-metastate-deg-14[panel a of Figure]).
2. *Interferon-Primed Antigen-Presenting Macrophages (`M14: CXCL9+STAT1+ M1-TAM`)*: Second-ranked responder meta-state ($hat(beta)_("meta") = +1.96$, `milopy` $overline("logFC") = +4.86$), defined by _CXCL9_ ($+2.42, t = +4.42$), _STAT1_ ($+1.90, t = +3.81$), _GBP1_ ($+1.39, t = +3.74$), and _IDO1_ ($+0.96, t = +3.06$), capturing the CXCR3-ligand myeloid recruitment hub.
3. *Progenitor/Stem-Like & Granzyme-K Effector-Memory $"CD8"^+$ T Cells (`M15: TCF7+IL7R+ Stem CD8` & `M34: GZMK+NKG7+ Tem CD8+`)*: Both precursor self-renewing `M15` ($hat(beta)_("meta") = +1.85$, `milopy` $overline("logFC") = +5.14$; markers _TCF7_ $+2.45$, _IL7R_ $+1.93$, _LEF1_ $+1.42$, _CCR7_ $+0.99$) and transitional cytotoxic `M34` ($hat(beta)_("meta") = +1.39$, `milopy` $overline("logFC") = +5.04$; markers _GZMK_ $+2.51, t = +3.54$, _NKG7_ $+1.99$, _CST7_ $+1.48$, _CCL5_ $+1.05$) co-enrich robustly with `M13`, reconciling the stem-like renewal (`TCF7+`) @sadefeldman2018defining and intratumoral neoantigen-reactive (`CXCL13+`) @vanderleunCD8CellStates2020 paradigms within a single unified reference.
4. *Tertiary Lymphoid Structure B Cells & Antibody-Secreting Plasma Cells (`M37: MS4A1+CD79A+ TLS B` & `M29: JCHAIN+MZB1+ Plasma`)*: `M37` ($hat(beta)_("meta") = +1.51$, `milopy` $overline("logFC") = +2.84$; _MS4A1_ $+2.48, t = +3.23$, _CD79A_ $+1.96, t = +3.07$, _CD19_ $+1.45$, _BANK1_ $+1.02$) and `M29` (_JCHAIN_ $+3.26, t = +2.16$, _MZB1_ $+2.73, t = +2.39$, _IGHG1_ $+1.76$, _SDC1_ $+1.56$) establish germinal-center and humoral effector support across melanoma, NSCLC, and bladder trials.

Conversely, checkpoint resistance at the non-responder pole (@fig-main-combined[panel f of Figure], @fig-metastate-forest-all42) is dominated by desmoplastic extracellular-matrix remodeling, myeloid suppression, and mesenchymal dedifferentiation:
- *TGF-$beta$-Activated Matrix & Myofibroblastic CAFs (`M23: FAP+TGFB1+ Mat. CAF` & `M17: ISLR+COL5A2+ myCAF`)*: `M23` exhibits the strongest pan-cancer resistance effect in both modalities ($hat(beta)_("meta") = -3.13$, `milopy` $overline("logFC") = -7.96$), marked by _FAP_ ($+2.42$, bulk $t = -4.24$), _TGFB1_ ($+1.90, t = -3.51$), _COL1A1_ ($+1.49, t = -3.42$), and _ITGA8_ ($+1.83, t = -2.84$), complemented by collagen-crosslinking `M17` ($hat(beta)_("meta") = -1.11$, `milopy` $overline("logFC") = -0.45$; _ISLR_ $+3.35$, _COL5A2_ $+2.82$, _ITGA11_ $+2.32$, _DIO2_ $+1.89$).
- *Effector Regulatory T Cells, Polymorphonuclear MDSCs & Mesenchymal Tumor States (`M21`, `M19`, `M26`)*: `M21` (`FOXP3+CTLA4+ Treg`: $hat(beta)_("meta") = -1.70, overline("logFC") = -3.41$; _FOXP3_, _CTLA4_, _CCDC80_, _SERPINE1_), `M19` (`S100A8+CXCL8+ MDSC`: $hat(beta)_("meta") = -1.50, overline("logFC") = -2.75$; _S100A8_, _CXCL8_, _GREM1_, _CLDN11_), and `M26` (`VIM+ZEB1+ EMT Tumor`: $hat(beta)_("meta") = -1.10, overline("logFC") = -2.17$; _VIM_, _ZEB1_, _NELL1_, _SNAI2_) consistently enrich in non-responders across melanoma, urothelial, renal, and pancreatic trials.

Expanding from the 14 extreme poles to the complete 42-state continuum (@fig-metastate-forest-all42, @fig-metastate-deg-all42) explains why `Consensus-Meta42` achieves superior predictive resolution over coarse cell-type signatures. Rather than lumping macrophages, B cells, CD4/CD8 T cells, or stromal fibroblasts into monolithic lineages whose opposing sub-states cancel out in bulk mixtures, the 42-state atlas resolves intra-lineage functional bifurcations across all `168` state-gene markers: `M14` (`CXCL9+STAT1+ M1-TAM`, $hat(beta)_("meta") = +1.96$) separates cleanly from lipid-associated/alternatively activated `M32` (`MRC1+FABP4+ M2-TAM`) and hypoxic myeloid niches; follicular `M37`/`M30` TLS B cells and `M29` plasma cells separate from resting naive B subsets; and intermediate homeostatic endothelial, perivascular, dendritic, natural killer (`M36: FCGR3A+GNLY+ NK`), and hypoxic epithelial (`M12: CA9+SLC2A1+ Hypox.`) states occupy a smooth, well-calibrated interior gradient ($|hat(beta)_("meta")| < 0.9$) where only 5 low-amplitude boundary states exhibit minor cross-modality sign fluctuations around zero. Cross-referencing the `Consensus-Meta42` myeloid continuum against the independent $363,315$-cell Coulton et al. pan-cancer `TAM` atlas ($n = 279,104$ tumor-infiltrating cells) @coultonUsingPancancerAtlas2024 independently replicated this functional bifurcation across both transcriptomic proximity and clinical checkpoint outcomes (@fig-metastate-deg-14[panel c of Figure], @fig-supp-s23, @fig-supp-s24, @fig-supp-s25). Specifically, Ward hierarchical biclustering across the $4$ `Consensus-Meta42` myeloid states achieving $p < 0.01$ (`**` or stronger) in bulk deconvolution (`M19` `****`, `M14` `****`, `M41` `***`, `M09` `***`) and the $4$ Coulton true `TAM` substates achieving $q < 0.01$ (`**` or stronger) in `CPI1000+` ($N = 1,446$) responder enrichment (`ECMMac` `****`, `IFNGMac` `****`, `IFNMac3` `****`, `ICIMac2` `**`) aligned every significant `Consensus-Meta42` myeloid state with its global `#1` closest Coulton `TAM` counterpart along a diagonal resistance-to-response trajectory (@fig-metastate-deg-14[panel c of Figure]): resistance-associated `M19` ($hat(beta)_("meta") = -1.50$, `****`) mapped to matrisome-remodeling `ECMMac` (`#1`, shared _COL3A1_, _POSTN_, _CCDC80_, _COL1A2_), the sole substate significantly enriched in `CPI1000+` non-responders ($q = 3.82 times 10^(-5)$, Pan-iAtlas macrophage-adjusted $hat(beta)_("ICB")^("adj") = -0.174, p = 0.034$); interferon-primed `M14` ($hat(beta)_("meta") = +1.96$, `****`) and migratory `M41` (`LAMP3+CCR7+ mregDC`, $hat(beta)_("meta") = +1.00$, `***`) both mapped to interferon-$gamma$-licensed `IFNGMac` (`#1`, shared _CXCL9_, _STAT1_, _IDO1_, _GBP1_; `CPI1000+` $q = 1.37 times 10^(-11)$, $hat(beta)_("ICB")^("adj") = +0.458, p = 1.68 times 10^(-6)$) and co-clustered with `IFNMac3` ($q = 1.79 times 10^(-8)$, $hat(beta)_("ICB")^("adj") = +0.177$); and responder-enriched `M09` ($hat(beta)_("meta") = +0.84$, `***`) mapped to lipid-associated `ICIMac2` (`#1`, shared _CCL18_, _APOC1_, _CHI3L1_, _SDS_; `CPI1000+` $q = 2.76 times 10^(-3)$), whereas homeostatic `M32` mapped to `CPI1000+`-neutral iron-recycling `21_HemeMac` and resident `1_MetM2Mac`/`0_AlvMac` ($q = 1.0$; @fig-supp-s23, @fig-supp-s24).

Extending this external cross-atlas synthesis to the lymphoid and stromal arms of the TME Triad across $1,280,163$ empirical single cells (`Zheng_TCell_Atlas` @zhengPancancerSinglecellLandscape2021, `Chu_TCell_Atlas` @chuPancancerCellAtlas2023, `Gao_CAF_Atlas` @gaoCrosstissueHumanFibroblast2024, `Cords_CAF_Atlas` @cordsCancerassociatedFibroblastPhenotypes2024 @cordsCancerassociatedFibroblastClassification2023, `Buechler_CAF_Atlas` @buechlerCrosstissueOrganizationFibroblast2021, `Shi_PanCancer_Atlas` @shiCrosstissueMulticellularCoordination2025, and `Coulton_TAM_Atlas` @coultonUsingPancancerAtlas2024) confirmed high-resolution ontology conservation and clinical outcome concordance across all three compartments (@fig-metastate-deg-14[panels c–e of Figure], @fig-supp-atlas-zheng-tcell, @fig-supp-atlas-chu-tcell, @fig-supp-atlas-shi-tnk, @fig-supp-atlas-gao-caf, @fig-supp-atlas-cords-caf, @fig-supp-atlas-buechler-caf, @fig-supp-atlas-shi-caf, @fig-supp-triad-all42). Across the unified $42$-state Triad matrix (@fig-supp-triad-all42), $74.2%$ ($23/31$, mean cognate-versus-off-target affinity margin $+0.134$) of `Consensus-Meta42` T/NK, Stromal/CAF, and Myeloid/TAM states assigned top-1 to their cognate external single-cell lineage, while independent external atlases exhibited tight reciprocal best-hit equivalences (`Gao c04_LRRC15_CTHRC1_myCAF` $<->$ `Shi S23_myCAF_ACTA2`, cosine $+0.819, rho = +0.860$; `Gao c05_PI16_MFAP5_advFib` $<->$ `Shi S01_Fb_PI16`, cosine $+0.817, rho = +0.824$, and `Buechler 2_PI16_advProgenitor`, $rho = +0.598$; `Zheng CD4.TNFRSF9+ Treg` $<->$ `Shi CD4T08_Treg_FOXP3`, cosine $+0.694, rho = +0.761$; `Zheng CD8.terminal Tex` $<->$ `Shi CD8T07_Tex_CXCL13`, cosine $+0.672, rho = +0.741$). Within the T/NK compartment (@fig-metastate-deg-14[panel d of Figure], @fig-supp-atlas-zheng-tcell, @fig-supp-atlas-chu-tcell, @fig-supp-atlas-shi-tnk), `Consensus-Meta42` effector-memory and cytotoxic niches aligned directly with their specialized pan-cancer counterparts (under strict one-to-one assignment: `M06` $<->$ `Zheng CD8.Temra` $+0.560$ & `Shi I01_CD16hiNK_CREM` $+0.489$; `M04` $<->$ `Zheng CD8.ZNF683+CXCR6+ Trm` $+0.405$ & `Shi CD8T02_Tem_GZMK` $+0.381$; `M24` $<->$ `Zheng CD4.IL21+ Tfh` $+0.492$; `M40` $<->$ `Zheng CD4.TNFRSF9+ Treg` $+0.540$ & `Chu CD4_eTreg` $+0.334$), mirrored across $11$ bulk ICB cohorts ($N = 1,058$) where pan-T-load-adjusted tissue-resident/transitional CD8 signatures (`Chu CD8_Trm_KLRC1` $hat(beta)_("ICB")^("adj") = +0.655, q = 6.77 times 10^(-3)$; `Chu CD8_t-Teff` $+0.809, q = 0.0327$; `Zheng CD8.ZNF683+CXCR6+ Trm` $+0.469$) enriched in responders whereas effector Tregs and resting naive/Th2 CD4 pools (`Chu CD4_eTreg` $-0.560, q = 0.0217$; `Zheng CD4.Tn` $-0.382, q = 0.0303$; `Chu CD4_Th2` $-0.904, q = 6.77 times 10^(-3)$) enriched in non-responders. Within the stromal compartment (@fig-metastate-deg-14[panel e of Figure], @fig-supp-atlas-gao-caf, @fig-supp-atlas-cords-caf, @fig-supp-atlas-buechler-caf, @fig-supp-atlas-shi-caf), `Consensus-Meta42` collagen-crosslinking `M17: ISLR+COL5A2+ myCAF` mapped to desmoplastic `Cords mCAF_MMP11_POSTN` ($+0.291$), `Gao c16_SFRP2_SFRP4_matFib` ($+0.292$), `Buechler 0_COL3A1_panCAF` ($+0.204$), and `Shi S04_Fb_CD9` ($+0.439$) under one-to-one assignment, while pan-stroma-adjusted clinical meta-analysis ($N = 1,058$) uncovered a sharp stromal bifurcation between responder-promoting interferon-licensed and `CCL19+` TLS fibroblastic reticular cells (`Cords ifnCAF_IDO1_CXCL9` $hat(beta)_("ICB")^("adj") = +0.410, q = 2.22 times 10^(-6)$; `Buechler 5_CCL19_FRC_TLS` $+0.363, q = 1.51 times 10^(-5)$; `Gao c20_CXCL12_CCL19_immunoCAF` $+0.317, q = 1.64 times 10^(-3)$) versus resistance-inducing `LRRC15+` TGF-$beta$ myofibroblasts (`Buechler 1_LRRC15_myCAF` $-0.269, q = 0.0269$), which attenuated objective responder rates among high-`Zheng CD8.c12.Tex.CXCL13` tumors from $46.5%$ (`Low myCAF`, $n = 213$) down to $32.9%$ (`High myCAF`, $n = 313$; replicated on `Cords mCAF` at $44.5% -> 34.3%$ and on `Gao c04 myCAF` at $42.0% -> 36.0%$; tabulated in the supplementary comparison report).


== 3.21 Reference-Tier Pareto Frontier, Calibrated Cellular Epistasis, and Zero-Leakage Stromal Gating
Synthesizing prediction accuracy (`ROC-AUC`) and cross-modality biological fidelity (meta-analytic concordance $r$) across all 13 references defined a clear *Reference-Tier Pareto Frontier* (@fig-pareto-rf-outliers a). Single-indication Tier 1A references with sharp lineage separation (such as `GSE207422` and `GSE233203`) anchored the high-concordance vertical boundary ($r = 0.980–0.987$) within matched histologies, whereas `Consensus-Meta42` uniquely occupied the pan-cancer predictive Pareto apex—simultaneously maintaining $r = +0.928$ cross-modality fidelity while delivering the highest multi-cohort discrimination (`mean ROC-AUC` $= 0.713$) at a compact, non-collinear dimensionality ($K = 42$ vs. $K = 355$ in `Unified-All9`).

Crucially, regressing pan-cancer Random Forest Gini feature importance $I_k^("RF")$ against univariate single-cell effect magnitude $|overline("logFC")_k|$ across all 42 states (@fig-pareto-rf-outliers b) exposed a striking mechanistic anomaly: the single highest-importance feature in the entire 42-state Random Forest was *`M17: ISLR+COL5A2+ myCAF`* ($I_("M17")^("RF") = 0.0312$), despite `M17` exhibiting only a modest marginal single-cell shift ($overline("logFC") = -0.45$). Alongside `M17`, five additional states—`M20` (`IL7R+CD40LG+ Trm`), `M04` (`GNLY+PRF1+ Eff. T`), `M29` (`JCHAIN+MZB1+ Plasma`), `M05` (`TRAF3+CD96+ Mem. T`), and `M30` (`MS4A1+CR2+ TLS B`)—lay far above the linear main-effect trendline, pointing to non-linear conditional gating in which stromal, myeloid, and mesenchymal barriers modulate effector efficacy.

Testing this hypothesis rigorously across $N = 1,015$ pan-cancer clinical trial biopsies requires confronting two methodological pitfalls on unblended cellular representations: compositional simplex closure on deconvolved centered log-ratios (`Pure CLR`, $alpha = 0.00$) and Type I error inflation under nonlinear logit saturation or post-selection outcome peeking (@fig-supp-s19, @fig-supp-s20, @fig-supp-s21). Evaluating all $binom(42, 2) = 861$ unordered pairs on $100%$ unblended `InstaPrism` `CLR` abundances (@fig-supp-s19) demonstrated that the *Leave-Two-Out Ridge-Nuisance, Quadratic-Guarded Logistic Saddlepoint Approximation (`L2O-Quad-Logit-SPA`)* eliminates the severe genomic-control inflation of naive two-state permutation testing ($lambda_("GC") = 0.95$ vs. $2.05$; $13/861$ pairs at $p_("SPA") < 0.01$ and $7/861$ at $p_("SPA") < 0.005$, led by `M03` $times$ `M12` at $z_("SPA") = +3.33, p_("SPA") = 8.7 times 10^(-4)$). However, because Aitchison closure ($sum_(k=1)^42 c_(n, k) = 0$) couples co-infiltrating lymphocyte subsets across whole-tumor cellularity, no individual pure-`CLR` pair survives 861-test multiplicity adjustment (minimum $q_("BH") = 0.48$). Benchmarking six non-hybrid epistasis strategies against a 42-state additive-plus-$z_k^2$ quadratic `Saturation Null B` (@fig-supp-s20 a, b) confirmed that while naive two-state `CLR` permutation (`Null B FPR` $= 44.5%$) and outcome-peeking marginal pre-screening (`Null B FPR` $= 14.2%$) suffer substantial false-positive inflation, `L2O-Quad-Logit-SPA` strategies preserve Type I error calibration and reveal a two-tier biological architecture of checkpoint blockade epistasis (@fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main):
1. *Tier I — Pan-Compartment Clinical TME Gating (`R_OOF` $times$ `NR_OOF`; @fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main a–e, g–i, @fig-supp-s22 a–d, @fig-pan-pair-grid)*: Evaluating honest 5-fold out-of-fold (`OOF`) composites (choosing strictly between unblended `Pure CLR` and `SC-Markers` by inner training-fold CV; `Null B FPR` $= 5.0%$, permuted-$y$ `FPR` $= 4.8%$) revealed that multi-compartment immune infiltration (`R_OOF`, anchored in `5/5` folds by cytotoxic T cells `M04`/`M25`/`M34`/`M33`, `M14` `CXCL9+STAT1+ M1-TAM`, and `B/Plasma` states; @fig-supp-s22 d)—and mirrored at single-cell resolution across the three cross-compartment arms of the host TME Triad (`T-NK cells` $<->$ `CAF` led by `M08` $times$ `M17`, $Z_("ACAT") = +4.14^(***)$; `Myeloid/Macrophages` $<->$ `CAF` led by `M17` $times$ `M42`, $Z_("ACAT") = -4.41^(***)$; and `T-NK cells` $<->$ `Myeloid/Macrophages` led by `M06` $times$ `M09`, $Z_("ACAT") = +3.39$; @fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main g–i, @fig-pan-pair-grid)—is conditionally gated by a multi-compartment stromal-myeloid-mesenchymal barrier (`NR_OOF`, anchored by `M19` `S100A8+CXCL8+ MDSC`, `M38` `TAN`, `M26` `VIM+ZEB1+ EMT`, and `M12` `CA9+SLC2A1+ Hypox.`). Unbinned continuous logistic models confirmed a steep monotonic `R_OOF` responder gradient in *Permissive Low-Barrier TME (`NR_OOF T1`)* biopsies (`OR` $= 1.46$ per SD, Wald $p = 9.3 times 10^(-5)$, $n = 343$) versus a flat trajectory in *Excluded High-Barrier TME (`NR_OOF T3`)* biopsies (`OR` $= 1.13$ per SD, $p = 0.45$, $n = 337$; @fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main a), alongside reciprocal continuous `NR_OOF` barrier suppression among `High-R` tumors (`OR` $= 0.68$ per SD, $p = 6.9 times 10^(-4)$, $n = 337$) versus `Low-R` (`OR` $= 0.95$ per SD, $p = 0.73$, $n = 343$; @fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main b). Across the joint two-dimensional $(R_("OOF"), "NR"_("OOF"))$ manifold and its four $2 times 2$ extreme-tertile corners ($n = 449$ corner patients across 9 trials; @fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main c, @fig-supp-s22 a, b), rising `R_OOF` (`T1` $->$ `T3`) elevated objective response by $+24.8%$ in Permissive TME ($26.7%$ [35/131] $->$ *$51.6%$* [66/128], `OR` $= 2.92$, Fisher $p = 4.5 times 10^(-5)$, trial-stratified `CMH` $p = 1.5 times 10^(-5)$, `BH` $q(5) = 0.00023$, `OOF` permutation $p = 0.0020$) while remaining blunted in Excluded TME ($25.0%$ [23/92] $->$ $27.6%$ [27/98], $Delta = +2.6%, p = 0.74$), yielding a $-24.0%$ stromal gate at `High R` ($51.6%$ vs. $27.6%$, Fisher $p = 3.7 times 10^(-4)$, `BH` $q(5) = 0.00037$) and significant $2 times 2$ Difference-in-Differences epistasis ($Delta Delta = +22.3%$, `OR` $= 2.56$, Wald $p = 0.0101$, `CMH` $p = 0.0117$, `OOF` permutation $p = 0.0140$), with concordant positive corner shifts across `4/4` solid-tumor lineages (`Melanoma` $+23.3% / +22.0%$, `Bladder` $+20.5% / +25.0%$, `Renal ccRCC` $+25.9% / +19.3%$, `PDAC + TNBC` $+40.0% / +34.5%$; @fig-supp-s22 c) and enriched complete responses (`CR` $= 16.4%$, `DCR` $= 76.6%$; @fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main d). Crucially, despite zero survival supervision during `OOF` training, orthogonal Kaplan–Meier Overall Survival (`OS`) validation (@fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main e) confirmed unreached median `OS` in `High-R / Permissive` patients versus `17.1` months in `Low-R / Permissive` (log-rank $p = 8.5 times 10^(-5)$) and `19.1` months in `High-R / Excluded` (log-rank $p = 3.1 times 10^(-4)$), with invariant survival between `High-R` and `Low-R` under stromal exclusion (`19.1` vs. `15.6` months, $p = 0.79$).
2. *Outcome-Leakage Audit Across Seven Stromal Gating Estimators (@fig-supp-s21 a–e)*: Auditing two in-sample baselines against five strictly zero-leakage $3 times 3$ regime estimators across $B = 500$ within-trial label permutations on unblended representations revealed severe positive permutation-null optimism in explicit response-residual orthogonalization ($EE[Delta_(T 3) | bold(y)_("perm")] = +39.9%$ vs. $+51.2%$ observed) and in-sample bulk-$hat(beta)$ weighting ($+11.0%$ vs. $+27.5%$). Conversely, all five strict zero-leakage estimators centered cleanly near $0%$ under permutation ($|EE[Delta_(T 3) | bold(y)_("perm")]| <= 3.2%$) while independently confirming stromal gating: Aitchison `ILR` cell-ontology balances on raw `InstaPrism` $bold(theta)$ (`Zero y`: $Delta_(T 3) = +6.3%$, trial-adjusted `FWL` $Delta = +9.2%, p_("perm") = 0.050$), external single-cell `milopy` priors on unblended `SC-Markers` (`Zero Bulk y`: $Delta_(T 3) = +18.2%, p_("perm") = 0.0020$), honest 5-fold `OOF` cross-fitting ($Delta_(T 3) = +24.0%, "FWL" Delta = +23.6%, p_("perm") = 0.0020$), nested 9-trial `LOCO` cross-fitting ($Delta_(T 3) = +16.2%, p_("perm") = 0.010$), and unsupervised rank-4 `NMF` ($Delta_(T 3) = +5.5%$).
3. *Tier II — Specific Non-T-Cell Pairwise Cellular Epistasis (`M29 Plasma` $times$ `M26 EMT`; @fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main f, @fig-supp-s20 b–d)*: Scanning all $M = 861$ individual cell-state pairs via `L2O-Quad-Logit-SPA` on unblended single-cell canonical 4-gene marker signatures (`SC-Markers`, $alpha = 1.00$, free of simplex closure; `Null B FPR` $= 9.5%$, permuted-$y$ `FPR` $= 5.1%$) pinpointed a specific non-T-cell interaction—humoral *`M29: JCHAIN+MZB1+ Plasma`* gated by mesenchymal *`M26: VIM+ZEB1+ EMT Tumor`*—as the sole pairwise interaction surviving genome-wide multiplicity ($z_("SPA") = -4.12$, $beta_(a b) = -0.314$ $[-0.464, -0.165]$, $p_("SPA") = 3.85 times 10^(-5)$, all-861 `BH` $q = 0.0332$, Westfall–Young `max-|Z|` $p_("FWER") = 0.0240$; sharpened five-fold to `BH` $q(160) = 0.0062$ under an outcome-blind 160-pair single-cell `milopy` bipartite screen; @fig-supp-s20 b), whereas every `T/NK`-cell state pair remained sub-threshold after controlling for the 40 background TME states ($q_("BH") >= 0.37$, n.s.). Unbinned continuous logistic dose-response modeling (@fig-epistasis-stromal-gating-main f) confirmed that rising `M29` humoral plasma score strongly elevates objective response in *Epithelial Low-`EMT` (`M26 T1`)* tumors (`OR` $= 1.78$ per SD, Wald $p = 2.4 times 10^(-6)$, $n = 343$) but is completely neutralized in *Mesenchymal High-`EMT` (`M26 T3`)* tumors (`OR` $= 1.00$ per SD, $p = 0.99$, n.s., $n = 337$). Consistently, across discrete $2 times 2$ extreme-tertile corners (@fig-supp-s20 d), `M29` enrichment (`T1` $->$ `T3`) more than doubled objective response in `Low-EMT` tumors from $22.4%$ (32/143) to *$54.0%$* (47/87, two-sided Fisher $p = 1.7 times 10^(-6)$) while remaining flat in `High-EMT` tumors ($27.4%$ [29/106] $->$ $28.9%$ [33/114], $p = 0.88$; high-`M29` `EMT` gate $Delta = +25.1%$, Fisher $p = 4.5 times 10^(-4)$), with directionally concordant negative interaction log-odds across all `4/4` solid-tumor lineages (`Melanoma` $z_("SPA") = -2.59$, `Urothelial Bladder` $z_("SPA") = -2.43$, `Renal ccRCC` $z_("SPA") = -1.24$, `PDAC + TNBC` $z_("SPA") = -0.92$; @fig-supp-s20 c).
